meta {
  name: rate-dish
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

put {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/guest/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/items/{{order_item_uid}}/review
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Guest-Token: {{guest_token}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "rating": 5,
    "tags": ["tasty", "worth_the_wait"]
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.status === 200 && res.body.data) {
    bru.setVar("review_uid", res.body.data.uid)
  }
}

tests {
  test("responds 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200)
  })

  test("echoes the rating back", function () {
    expect(res.body.data.rating).to.equal(5)
    expect(res.body.data.tags).to.deep.equal(["tasty", "worth_the_wait"])
  })

  test("carries no comment, because none was sent", function () {
    // The whole point of the interaction: a complete review with no prose in it. A diner who
    // taps a star and nothing else has submitted everything this feature asks for.
    expect(res.body.data.comment).to.be.undefined
  })
}

docs {
  # Rate a dish

Sets `review_uid`.

**PUT, not POST, and that is the product decision showing through the verb.** The diner rates with
one tap and there is no Submit button, so every tap has to be safe to repeat. Send this request
twice: the second call returns the same `uid` and does not create a second review. The unique index
on `order_item_id` is what guarantees that -- no idempotency key is involved, unlike order
placement, because this endpoint cannot create a second row in the first place.

`rating` is the only required field. `tags` come from a closed vocabulary and `comment` is capped at
280 characters; both are optional, and a client that never sends either is not sending a partial
review.

Worth provoking:
- `"rating": 6` or `0` -> `TX_REV_002` (422)
- `"tags": ["delicious"]` -> `TX_REV_003` (422). An unrecognised tag is rejected rather than
  silently dropped: the value of a fixed vocabulary is that every stored tag can be counted, and a
  quietly discarded typo produces a count nobody can account for.
- an item uid from another order -> `TX_REV_004` (404), matching the 404 on a foreign order uid
- running this before `admin-orders / mark-served` -> `TX_REV_001` (409)
}
